{"type": "FeatureCollection", "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Baltimore and Ohio Railroad", "line": "Baltimore and Ohio: Main Stem", "id": "bo-mainstem-baltimore-wheeling", "color": "#00447C", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 40, "speed_source": "estimated: mid-19th-century mountain-grade steam service; unsourced. Route geometry from Jeremy Atack's Historical Railroad GIS dataset (Vanderbilt), 1826-1911, filtered to 'Baltimore & Ohio' / 'Baltimore and Ohio' segments and stitched into a single path (real digitized track alignment, not hand-plotted waypoints).", "segments": [{"start": 1853, "end": 1900, "headway_min": 360, "note": "Chartered Feb 28, 1827 - America's first common-carrier railroad. Charles Carroll of Carrollton (the last surviving signer of the Declaration of Independence) laid the first stone July 4, 1828. Opened to Ellicott's Mills (13 miles) May 24, 1830 - the country's first regularly scheduled passenger/freight rail service - then followed the Potomac River valley through Frederick and Harpers Ferry (1834/1837), reaching Martinsburg and Hancock in May/June 1842 and Cumberland on Nov 5, 1842 (the end of the line for nearly a decade). The final, hardest push west crossed the Alleghenies via Piedmont (1851) and the Backbone Mountain summit near Altamont and Terra Alta, then Grafton and Fairmont (June 1852), finally reaching the Ohio River at Wheeling in late December 1852/Jan 1, 1853 - 379 miles from Baltimore and nearly 25 years after construction began, requiring 11 tunnels and over 100 bridges. This fulfilled the original charter, giving Baltimore a rail route to western trade competing with New York's Erie Canal. The Washington Branch (Relay-Washington, DC) opened in 1835, joining the main stem at Relay - not separately plotted here. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baltimore_and_Ohio_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1900, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 180, "note": "Cumberland was also the junction where B&O's Pittsburgh-Chicago route (used by the Capitol Limited from 1923 - see baltimore_and_ohio_capitol_limited.geojson) diverged from this original Wheeling-bound main stem. Passenger service on this corridor wound down through the mid-20th century like the rest of the B&O system; all B&O intercity passenger service ended May 1, 1971 with Amtrak's creation, and this original main stem was not among the routes Amtrak initially retained. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baltimore_and_Ohio_Railroad"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-76.62080645543226, 39.2744560732667], [-76.62963383685953, 39.276177022250124], [-76.66378392176904, 39.25277295254087], [-76.66597390459286, 39.24243606486438], [-76.67824929532654, 39.23185015794166], [-76.7134756012239, 39.223255106593], [-76.73838670378031, 39.232806843244546], [-76.75357741795376, 39.24666956902512], [-76.79422854079442, 39.26581126134047], [-76.78141066309568, 39.29602015043383], [-76.79622809715651, 39.30394700817193], [-76.79453419058646, 39.313738957138014], [-76.8256399715882, 39.3199226902524], [-76.85238151261832, 39.31583253078661], [-76.86224691481024, 39.328379340112335], [-76.87633128536518, 39.33193819348386], [-76.87723143591411, 39.34083409462403], [-76.88972089366841, 39.35246086193078], [-76.92255164877155, 39.35261656690906], [-76.94630233006329, 39.361276235227294], [-76.95990558244989, 39.35742716313428], [-76.97